artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let an organization be modeled as a (possibly time-varying) network G = (V, E), where V = {1, ..., N} is a set of agents and E ⊆ V × V is a set of directed or undirected interaction links, optionally weighted by w: E → R_{>0}. In the temporal case, G is replaced by a sequence (G_t)_{t ∈ T} of graphs on V, or by a stream of timestamped edges {(i_k, j_k, t_k)}.

To each agent i ∈ V we wish to assign an importance score r_i ∈ R_{≥0} that reflects features such as hierarchical position, reputation, embedding in the organization, and access to knowledge. Several candidate proxies derived purely from the (temporal) topology are available, including:
  (i) hierarchical level in a partial order induced from directed links;
  (ii) eigenvector centrality, i.e. the i-th entry of the leading eigenvector of the adjacency matrix A of G;
  (iii) coreness, i.e. the largest k such that i belongs to the k-core of G;
  (iv) weighted k-core centrality, defined using a generalized degeneracy ordering on the weighted graph;
  (v) temporal-network centralities, such as temporal closeness, temporal betweenness, or eigenvector-type centralities computed on the time-ordered supra-adjacency matrix.

Problem. Propose a principled, general framework that, given only the topological (and, where applicable, temporal) data of G, assigns to each agent i an importance value r_i ∈ R_{≥0} that captures the multidimensional notion of importance described above. Specifically:
  (a) Define a set of axioms or invariance properties (e.g. behavior under graph isomorphism, monotonicity in added edges, locality, consistency under time aggregation) that any such importance measure r should satisfy.
  (b) Exhibit a measure r — possibly a function r = F(c^{(1)}, ..., c^{(m)}) of the centrality proxies (i)–(v) — that satisfies these axioms, and prove its uniqueness or characterize the family of measures satisfying them.
  (c) Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on the network data under which r is well-defined and identifiable from topological information alone, and quantify how r changes under perturbations of the network (robustness/stability).

State and prove the existence, uniqueness (or non-uniqueness), and stability properties of such a topological/temporal importance measure, or prove that no measure satisfying the stated axioms can exist.
